WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.102
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.102 --> 00:00:09.869
As I started speaking, what was
the first thing you noticed about me?

00:00:09.869 --> 00:00:14.912
Was it my charming smile, my charismatic

00:00:14.912 --> 00:00:19.241
personality, or was it my accent?

00:00:19.241 --> 00:00:22.937
Having lived in the US now for
almost three years,

00:00:22.937 --> 00:00:26.820
I realized that for
a lot of people, it is my accent.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:33.610
I come from Pakistan, which is why I
have an unmistakable South Asian accent.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:36.383
I've been fascinated by accents for
many years.

00:00:36.383 --> 00:00:39.549
First because, well, we all have one.

00:00:39.549 --> 00:00:44.551
Second, I feel strongly about how
a person's unique accent can trigger

00:00:44.551 --> 00:00:50.210
people's conscious and unconscious biases
and lead to accent discrimination.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:55.826
Research has shown that we're less
likely to believe something if it is

00:00:55.826 --> 00:00:57.240
said with a foreign accent.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:03.322
These negative judgments are the result of
the additional effort their brains must

00:01:03.322 --> 00:01:05.100
make to process foreign speech.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.456
Our brains then shift the blame for

00:01:08.456 --> 00:01:11.850
this additional effort on to
the truthfulness of the speaker.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.008
And it can trigger frustration.

00:01:16.008 --> 00:01:20.394
About a year ago, I was on the phone
with my health insurance company for

00:01:20.394 --> 00:01:23.624
some information I needed
before an upcoming trip.

00:01:23.624 --> 00:01:26.741
I had been on the call for almost an hour,

00:01:26.741 --> 00:01:32.543
hopping from one call agent to another
without any solution to my problem.

00:01:32.543 --> 00:01:34.850
I was becoming more and more impatient.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.387
I was then redirected for the fourth time.

00:01:39.387 --> 00:01:43.352
And as the new call
agent started speaking,

00:01:43.352 --> 00:01:47.962
I instantly noticed
a distinct East Asian accent,

00:01:47.962 --> 00:01:53.125
perhaps Chinese or Korean,
and I exploded internally.

00:01:53.125 --> 00:01:55.080
I don't need this accent right now.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.660
I didn't say this to her, of course,
but that's what I thought.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.339
My frustration was caused
by my complete inability

00:02:04.339 --> 00:02:06.590
to understand anything she was saying.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:10.308
And then I stopped.

00:02:10.308 --> 00:02:16.034
At that point, it hit me I was
doing exactly what I've been so

00:02:16.034 --> 00:02:18.178
strongly opposed to.

00:02:18.178 --> 00:02:24.686
I was allowing someone's accent
to influence how I saw them,

00:02:24.686 --> 00:02:29.329
how I listened to them,
how I treated them.

00:02:29.329 --> 00:02:34.004
That day, I was able to learn
what I felt deeply about for

00:02:34.004 --> 00:02:36.600
many years, accent empathy.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:41.838
I define accent empathy as thinking
deeper about how a person's

00:02:41.838 --> 00:02:46.883
unique accent is a manifestation
of their lived experience,

00:02:46.883 --> 00:02:50.970
of their cultural identity,
of their humanity.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:55.453
Growing up in Pakistan, I saw
firsthand how perfecting your English

00:02:55.453 --> 00:02:59.871
proficiency was seen as much more
than just language acquisition.

00:02:59.871 --> 00:03:05.770
It was a symbol of status,
of ability, of sophistication.

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:09.090
This is the legacy of 200
years of British colonialism.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:14.941
Mastery of spoken English was what
separated the colonial masters

00:03:14.941 --> 00:03:16.733
from their Indian subjects.

00:03:16.733 --> 00:03:19.185
This gave rise to the brown Sahibs,

00:03:19.185 --> 00:03:24.418
Indians who yearn to imitate the British
in language, dress or lifestyle,

00:03:24.418 --> 00:03:29.261
because doing so conferred patronage
from the colonial government.

00:03:29.261 --> 00:03:33.837
Even years after the British
left the Indian subcontinent,

00:03:33.837 --> 00:03:38.782
this English centered indoctrination
was still alive and well.

00:03:38.782 --> 00:03:43.741
I grew up seeing adults and
children giggle at and even openly insult

00:03:43.741 --> 00:03:48.620
people who spoke broken English or
mispronounce an English word.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:55.852
And the opposite was also true, speaking
fluent with perfect pronunciation and

00:03:55.852 --> 00:03:58.060
good accents give you prestige.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:03.011
My high school debate coach
once said to our entire team,

00:04:03.011 --> 00:04:05.810
all of you need to fix your
accent except Mansoor.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:11.280
I'd be lying if I said I didn't
feel immense pride at that point.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:16.508
It may seem to be misplaced
pride in retrospect, but

00:04:16.508 --> 00:04:19.430
then in that moment, my pride was real.

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:27.294
Yet this reinforced within me a certain
toxic complex and insecurity.

00:04:27.294 --> 00:04:28.987
I had to protect my accent.

00:04:30.487 --> 00:04:35.450
As I traveled to places outside
of Pakistan, I began to realize

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:40.520
that mastery of a language or
accent is not a sign of competence.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:45.410
I began to accept my accent as
part of who I am, of my identity.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:51.219
Then I came to the US and suddenly
found myself in the odd position of

00:04:51.219 --> 00:04:53.859
the guy with the different foreign accent.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.091
Sometimes when I spoke in Zoom meetings,
I was met with complete silence.

00:05:03.109 --> 00:05:05.777
The group, all native English speakers,

00:05:05.777 --> 00:05:08.750
would move on to speaking
amongst themselves.

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:11.520
Why was this happening?

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:15.470
Did they disagree with
the substance of what I'd said?

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:17.720
Was it Zoom fatigue?

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:19.710
Was it my accent?

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:21.810
I could only speculate.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.190
But this did take a toll on me.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:30.390
I felt overlooked, dismissed, invisible.

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:35.841
Fortunately for me,
by this stage of my life,

00:05:35.841 --> 00:05:39.238
I genuinely began to
cherish my unique accent.

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:42.754
I'm not making a political
statement of any sort.

00:05:42.754 --> 00:05:49.552
I truly cherish my Pakistani accent as a
symbol of my life journey and my identity,

00:05:49.552 --> 00:05:55.971
I value it as an expression of love for
my family, my grandparents, my home.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.370
But accent discrimination is real.

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:06.171
According to research at
Curtin University in Australia,

00:06:06.171 --> 00:06:11.584
there is a hierarchy that places American,
British, and Australian English

00:06:11.584 --> 00:06:16.603
accent at the top, views European
accented English as cute or stylish.

00:06:16.603 --> 00:06:20.400
But Asian or
African accents as ugly and unpleasant.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:27.650
The UK is known to view specific accents
as working class versus sophisticated.

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:31.179
Former British Prime Minister,

00:06:31.179 --> 00:06:35.150
Margaret Thatcher had to lose the
Lincolnshire accent to sound more posh.

00:06:37.519 --> 00:06:41.485
In the US,
the story is a bit more nuanced.

00:06:41.485 --> 00:06:43.952
Lauren Rivera in her book, Pedigree,

00:06:43.952 --> 00:06:48.449
writes that accents play a role in
shaping recruitment at elite firms.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:51.894
According to Rivera,

00:06:51.894 --> 00:06:57.177
interviewers frequently questioned the
polish of foreign students of all races,

00:06:57.177 --> 00:07:02.390
complaining that most candidates' accents
made them difficult to understand.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:09.200
Exceptions were those with pure British,
German or Scandinavian accents.

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:13.200
This is the accent hierarchy at work.

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:18.488
There's also anti black
racism in linguistics,

00:07:18.488 --> 00:07:23.088
something that even former First Lady
Michelle Obama had to contend with.

00:07:23.088 --> 00:07:27.420
And she's a Harvard educated lawyer.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:32.454
But I believe that we owe it to ourselves,
and to each other,

00:07:32.454 --> 00:07:37.886
to put in that little extra effort
to move beyond any cognitive and

00:07:37.886 --> 00:07:42.170
intellectual laziness that
might be stifling us.

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:47.090
So the next time you find yourself
struggling to engage with someone having

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:50.381
an unfamiliar accent,
I challenge you to pause.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:53.347
Take a deep breath.

00:07:55.862 --> 00:08:00.390
Ask yourself,
why am I having such a reflexive reaction?

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:04.160
Is it just because the accent
is different from my own?

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:10.702
Think about what your accent
says about you, your family,

00:08:10.702 --> 00:08:16.401
your festivals, your aspirations,
your struggles, your identity.

00:08:16.401 --> 00:08:20.210
Do you really want to rob
someone of the same just so

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:23.577
you can save a few
seconds of mental energy.

00:08:23.577 --> 00:08:28.065
That, to me, is accent empathy in action.

00:08:28.065 --> 00:08:33.019
And if you truly aspire to change
lives and change organizations in

00:08:33.019 --> 00:08:37.983
an increasingly interconnected world,
we need accent empathy.

00:08:37.983 --> 00:08:38.731
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:38.731 --> 00:08:43.237
[MUSIC]

